Title: Candidate catalog for Type II and Type III long-period compact-object candidate binaries
Authors: Zhou, J.; Gu, W.-M.
Table: Catalog of Type II and Type III Long-period Binary Candidates for Compact-object Follow-up Observations

Description:
  Table 2 is provided as a machine-readable table in fixed-width ASCII format. The machine-  readable file is `tab2.txt`, with 17 records, and contains the catalog of Type II and Type III long-period compact-object candidate binaries. The table includes Gaia DR3 source identifiers, RUWE values, coordinates, parallaxes and their formal uncertainties, follow-up priority types, stellar categories of the visible primaries, primary-star masses and uncertainties, orbital periods, RV semi-amplitudes, eccentricities, binary mass functions, minimum companion masses, expected companion-to-primary luminosity ratios, and minimum mass ratios. No figure sets, animated figures, data-behind-the-figures files, interactive figures, or other digital assets are included.

  This machine-readable table provides the Gaia DR3 source identifiers,
  astrometric quality indicators, parallaxes, stellar parameters, orbital solutions,
  minimum companion masses, luminosity ratios, and follow-up priority
  classes for the 17 Type II and Type III long-period compact-object
  candidate binaries discussed in the paper. The two Type I candidates
  are listed separately in Table 1.

File Summary:
  FileName   Lrecl  Records  Explanations
  tab2.txt   197    17       Type II and Type III candidate catalog

Byte-by-byte Description of file: apjaeaa3at2_mrt.txt
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Bytes     Format   Units    Label          Explanations
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1-19      I19      ---      GaiaDR3        Gaia DR3 source identifier
21-25     F5.3     ---      RUWE           Gaia DR3 renormalised unit weight error
27-36     F10.6    deg      RAdeg          Right ascension in decimal degrees
38-46     F9.6     deg      DEdeg          Declination in decimal degrees
48-54     F7.4     mas      Plx            Gaia DR3 parallax
56-61     F6.4     mas      e_Plx          Formal uncertainty of Gaia DR3 parallax
63-65     A3       ---      Type           Follow-up priority type: II or III
67-81     A15      ---      StellarType    Stellar category of the visible primary
83-86     F4.2     Msun     M1             Primary-star mass from SED fitting
88-91     F4.2     Msun     e_M1_up        Upper uncertainty of M1
93-96     F4.2     Msun     e_M1_low       Lower uncertainty of M1
98-104    F7.1     d        Porb           Orbital period from RV modeling
106-111   F6.2     d        e_Porb_up      Upper uncertainty of Porb
113-118   F6.2     d        e_Porb_low     Lower uncertainty of Porb
120-124   F5.2     km/s     K1             RV semi-amplitude of the visible primary
126-129   F4.2     km/s     e_K1_up        Upper uncertainty of K1
131-134   F4.2     km/s     e_K1_low       Lower uncertainty of K1
136-140   F5.3     ---      ecc            Orbital eccentricity
142-146   F5.3     ---      e_ecc_up       Upper uncertainty of eccentricity
148-152   F5.3     ---      e_ecc_low      Lower uncertainty of eccentricity
154-158   F5.3     Msun     fM             Binary mass function
160-164   F5.3     Msun     e_fM_up        Upper uncertainty of fM
166-170   F5.3     Msun     e_fM_low       Lower uncertainty of fM
172-175   F4.2     Msun     M2min          Minimum companion mass assuming an edge-on orbit
177-180   F4.2     Msun     e_M2min_up     Upper uncertainty of M2min
182-185   F4.2     Msun     e_M2min_low    Lower uncertainty of M2min
187-191   F5.3     ---      L2_L1          Expected companion-to-primary luminosity ratio under the main-sequence assumption
193-197   F5.3     ---      q              Minimum mass ratio, M2min/M1
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Notes:
  Plx is the Gaia DR3 catalog parallax, and e_Plx is its formal uncertainty.
  M2min is computed assuming an edge-on orbit (i = 90 deg).
  L2_L1 is the expected companion-to-primary luminosity ratio under
  the main-sequence companion assumption.
  Type is a follow-up priority type, not a definitive physical
  classification.
  The two Type I candidates are presented in Table 1 and are not
  duplicated here.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

